CAT = "sgsecGuard"; SEG = "GRD"
# Guard and patrol and access control: PLRD refresher e-quiz training package (summary of
# SEC-SOP-1007-1.1 Guard and Patrol), the Conduct Regulations 2009 (regs 4 and 7) and the PSIA
# section 2 definition of screening.
Q = [
("An officer takes over the guardhouse at the start of a shift. What is checked against the equipment checklist?",
 "Every item handed over", ["The tenants' parking passes", "Last week's visitor log", "The previous officer's bag"],
 "Taking over duty starts with accounting for each item on the equipment checklist and organising it, keeping the post clean, and following up pending issues from earlier shifts."),
("During handover, the outgoing officer mentions a broken side gate reported overnight. The incoming officer should:",
 "Follow it up", ["Leave it for the day manager", "Note it only if asked again", "Wait for a second complaint"],
 "Taking over duty includes noting and following up pending issues and incidents of interest from previous shifts, so problems are not lost between shifts."),
("Why should officers get to know the tenants and staff of their guarded premises?",
 "It keeps the site safe and secure", ["It lets them skip pass checks", "It earns gifts at festive seasons", "It helps them sell extra services"],
 "Recognising tenants and staff helps keep a safe, secure and good working environment, because a stranger is noticed sooner."),
("A delivery driver's goods do not match the delivery order. What may the officer do?",
 "Reject the delivery", ["Accept and sign for it anyway", "Store it in the guardhouse", "Open the boxes to check them"],
 "The PLRD package says to ask for the correct delivery order, or reject the delivery, when goods do not tally with the order."),
("A visitor arrives with no appointment. Which detail should the officer confirm before issuing a pass?",
 "The purpose of the visit", ["The visitor's home address", "The visitor's nationality", "The visitor's job title"],
 "Before entry, the officer confirms why the visitor is there, the visitor's company and photo ID, records the entry and issues a visitor pass."),
("A visitor drives in and is given a vehicle pass. Where should the visitor display it?",
 "On the dashboard", ["In the glove box", "On the back seat", "In the car boot"],
 "Visitors with vehicles are reminded to place the pass visibly on the dashboard so patrolling officers can see the car is authorised."),
("After verifying a driver's identity at the barrier, what else should the officer do before letting the car in?",
 "Check it for suspicious items", ["Inspect its tyres for wear", "Ask for its insurance papers", "Check the engine number"],
 "For visitors with vehicles, the officer verifies the visitor's identity and then checks the vehicle for suspicious equipment before allowing entry."),
("The client's site orders require bags to be searched as people leave. A new officer asks if he may do it. He should:",
 "Search as instructed", ["Refuse, as only police search", "Search only contract workers", "Search only after office hours"],
 "The PLRD package says that where the company requires persons entering or leaving to be searched by security officers, the officer follows the company's instruction."),
("A Facilities Manager asks for the pump room key. What should the officer ask to see first?",
 "The manager's staff pass", ["The pump's service record", "A letter from a tenant", "The manager's work order"],
 "Key control begins with identification: request the staff pass, then check the key list to confirm the person may enter that room."),
("Before issuing a restricted key, against what should the officer check the requester's name?",
 "The authorised key list", ["Last week's visitor log", "The cleaners' duty roster", "The CCTV camera schedule"],
 "The officer verifies against the personnel list for that key to confirm the person is authorised before any key leaves the guardhouse."),
("When issuing a key, where is the movement recorded and signed?",
 "Key Movement Book", ["Lost and Found Book", "Occurrence Book only", "The visitor pass"],
 "The officer makes an entry in the Key Movement Book and has the collector sign it before handing over the key."),
("A contractor returns the plant room key after work. What should the officer check before the contractor leaves?",
 "That nothing is missing or left", ["That he signs a feedback form", "That his van is parked legally", "That he leaves by the back gate"],
 "After a contractor's work, the officer checks the room for missing equipment and makes sure nothing has been left behind."),
("A person banned from the premises pushes past the gate. What should the officer do?",
 "Detain him and get backup", ["Let him in and follow him", "Note his name and do nothing", "Lock the gate behind him"],
 "The PLRD scenario calls for detaining the banned person at the guardhouse, calling the Control Room for backup, then informing management and recording the incident."),
("A visitor hands in a wallet found in the car park. How should the officer check its contents?",
 "In the finder's presence", ["Alone in the back office", "After the finder has left", "Only if cash can be seen"],
 "The officer politely tells the finder the contents will be checked in their presence and invites them to witness it."),
("Where should details of a found wallet and its contents be recorded?",
 "Lost and Found Book", ["Key Movement Book", "Guardhouse whiteboard", "A personal notebook"],
 "Found property is recorded in the Lost and Found Book, including the wallet's colour and contents, and the finder is asked to check and sign."),
("After recording found property, where should the officer keep it?",
 "Under lock and key", ["On the guardhouse counter", "In the officer's pocket", "In the tenant mailbox"],
 "The wallet and its contents are kept in a safe place under lock and key until the owner claims them."),
("A caller says he is a journalist and asks how many CCTV cameras the building has. The officer should:",
 "Decline to answer", ["Give a rough estimate", "Read out the full list", "Ask him to visit and count"],
 "Camera locations and numbers are security information. The officer politely says they are not authorised to answer and offers to pass the call or a message to the supervisor."),
("If the supervisor is unavailable when a reporter calls, what should the officer offer?",
 "To take down a message", ["To email the site plans", "To meet the reporter later", "To give the figures tomorrow"],
 "The officer asks whether the caller wants to leave a message, records the date, time, name, media company, contact number and message, and passes it to the supervisor."),
("Under the Conduct Regulations, which may an officer NOT tell an outsider without consent or legal authority?",
 "How many officers are deployed", ["The building's postal address", "The visiting hours on the notice", "The way to the nearest MRT"],
 "Regulation 4(2) bars an officer from publishing information on the security system, the physical layout, the number and type of officers deployed, or other security arrangements."),
("Who may an officer lawfully tell about the site's security system under regulation 4(3)?",
 "The owner of the premises", ["A neighbour who is curious", "A friend at another agency", "A follower on social media"],
 "The ban in regulation 4 does not apply to sharing with an owner or lawful occupier, with their consent, when required by law, or as needed to prevent serious violence or an offence."),
("A visitor leaving has a company laptop in his bag. What should the officer ask for?",
 "A gate pass for it", ["His name card", "The laptop's password", "A shop receipt"],
 "The PLRD scenario says to ask whether the visitor has a gate pass or management permission to take the item out before doing anything else."),
("The visitor has no permission to remove the company item. What is the officer's next step?",
 "Seize it and detain him", ["Let him go and email HR", "Photograph it and wave him off", "Ask him to return it later"],
 "Without permission, the officer seizes the item, detains the visitor, keeps the item secure and calls the police, then hands both over when they arrive."),
("At premises covered by Conduct Regulation 7, an officer lets in a stranger without the occupier's authorisation. This is:",
 "An offence if knowing or negligent", ["Fine if the stranger is polite", "Allowed during the lunch rush", "Only a breach of house rules"],
 "Regulation 7(1)(a) bars an officer responsible for the premises it covers from knowingly or negligently letting a person enter without the owner's or occupier's authorisation."),
("At such premises, an officer stops an unauthorised entrant from leaving. What must happen next?",
 "Prompt handover to the police", ["Release after a firm warning", "Holding him until the shift ends", "A lift home in the site van"],
 "Regulation 7(2) requires an officer who prevents such a person from leaving to hand them over to a police officer without unnecessary delay."),
("Someone is smoking in a no-smoking area of the building. What should the officer do first?",
 "Ask them to stop", ["Fine them on the spot", "Take their cigarettes", "Ignore it unless asked"],
 "The officer stops the person smoking, explains it is a no-smoking zone, directs them to the smoking area, and informs the supervisor and records it if required."),
("The guardhouse phone rings while the officer is on a break inside. What should the officer do?",
 "Answer it promptly", ["Let it ring out", "Unplug it for quiet", "Answer known numbers"],
 "The PLRD package says to answer the guardhouse phone without delay and offer help where possible, checking with the supervisor if unsure."),
("A resident calls the guardhouse to report a fire. Which should the officer get from the caller?",
 "Where the fire is", ["The resident's car plate", "When the flat was bought", "The caller's employer"],
 "The officer stays calm and gathers information such as the type and location of the fire and the number injured, tells the caller SCDF will be called, and takes the caller's particulars."),
("Which screening method in the PSIA's definition involves no touching of the person?",
 "A walk-through detector", ["A frisk of outer clothing", "A hand search of a handbag", "A pat-down of the arms"],
 "Section 2 of the PSIA counts as screening a search by equipment designed to work without touching the person or their property, a frisk search, and a physical search of property not being worn."),
]
